{:edb/db-before-t 154 :edb/db-after-t 155 :edb/tx-data [[13194139533467 50 #inst "2026-10-10T23:49:12.435Z" 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186059807 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396209 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186059855 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396243 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186068866 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396024 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186078396 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396079 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186078396 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396106 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186078397 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396072 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186079067 1068 #edb/ref 17592186077150 13194139533467 17592186045585 false] [17592186079067 1068 #edb/ref 17592186396100 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186079067 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186079067 1071 "Find the center and radius of the circle whose equation is $x^{2} + y^{2} + 4x - 6y = 3$." 13194139533467 17592186045585 false] [17592186079067 1071 "Find the center and radius of the circle whose equation is ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+4x-6y=3.$" 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186079067 1074 "To find the center and radius of the circle, we need to group $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the squares.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+4x-6y & =3 \\\\ ({x}^{2}+4x)+({y}^{2}-6y) & =3 \\\\ ({x}^{2}+2\\cdot 2x+{2}^{2})+({y}^{2}-2\\cdot 3y+{3}^{2}) & =3+{2}^{2}+{3}^{2} \\\\ ({x}^{2}+2\\cdot 2x+4)+({y}^{2}-2\\cdot 3y+9) & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can factor the perfect squares and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+2)^{2}+(y-3)^{2} & ={4}^{2} \\\\ (x-(-2))^{2}+(y-3)^{2} & ={4}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the circle is centered at $(-2,3)$ and its radius is $4.$" 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186087664 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396086 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186087664 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396093 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186087702 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396168 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186087704 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396161 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186087708 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396175 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186087731 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396147 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186087732 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396140 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186087734 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396154 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186087734 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396236 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186087752 1038 #edb/ref 17592186395997 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186087752 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396182 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186087757 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396004 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186087757 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396189 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186087757 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396196 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186087844 1038 #edb/ref 17592186395977 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186087847 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396126 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186087847 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396133 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186087851 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396113 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186088283 1068 #edb/ref 17592186086322 13194139533467 17592186045585 false] [17592186088283 1068 #edb/ref 17592186396120 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186088283 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186088283 1071 "Determine the horizontal asymptote of $f(x) = \\frac{x^{3} + 3}{4x^{2} - 6x}$." 13194139533467 17592186045585 false] [17592186088283 1071 "Determine the horizontal asymptote of $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{3}+3}{4{x}^{2}-6x}.$" 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186088283 1074 "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{2}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{3}+3}{4{x}^{2}-6x} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{6x}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}})}{(4-\\frac{6}{x})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}$ and $\\frac{6}{x}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(x+\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}})}{(4-\\frac{6}{x})} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{x+0}{4-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is not a constant, which means that $y=f(x)$ does not have a horizontal asymptote." 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186088423 1068 #edb/ref 17592186087161 13194139533467 17592186045585 false] [17592186088423 1068 #edb/ref 17592186396203 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186088423 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186088423 1071 "Let\n$T: \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}y \\\\ x\\end{bmatrix}$.\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $T(kv) = kT(v)$ for any real $2 \\cdot 1$ column vector $v$ and real scalar $k$\n2. $T(v_{1} + v_{2}) = T(v_{1}) + T(v_{2})$ for any real $2 \\cdot 1$ column vectors $v_{1}$ and $v_{2}$\n3. $T$ is a linear transformation" 13194139533467 17592186045585 false] [17592186088423 1071 "Let \n$T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}y \\\\ x\\end{aligned}].$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $T(kv)=kT(v)$ for any real $2\\times 1$ column vector $v$ and real scalar $k$\nII. $T({v}_{1}+{v}_{2})=T({v}_{1})+T({v}_{2})$ for any real $2\\times 1$ column vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$\nIII. $T$ is a linear transformation" 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186088423 1074 "A linear transformation $T$ satisfies two properties:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & T[\\begin{aligned}kx \\\\ ky\\end{aligned}]=kT[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}], \\\\  & T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Checking the first condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T[\\begin{aligned}kx \\\\ ky\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}ky \\\\ kx\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =k[\\begin{aligned}y \\\\ x\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =kT[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Checking the second condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}+{y}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{1}+{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{T}$ is a linear transformation.\n\nAs a result, all three statements are true." 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186088448 1068 #edb/ref 17592186087311 13194139533467 17592186045585 false] [17592186088448 1068 #edb/ref 17592186396223 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186088448 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186088448 1071 "Calculate the $x$-intercepts of the circle $(x + 3)^{2} + (y - 2)^{2} = 3$" 13194139533467 17592186045585 false] [17592186088448 1071 "Calculate the $x$-intercepts of the circle $(x+3)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}=3$" 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186088448 1074 "We must have $y=0$ at the $x$-intercepts.  \n\nSo, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x,$ if possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)^{2}+(y-2)^{2} & =3 \\\\ (x+3)^{2}+(0-2)^{2} & =3 \\\\ (x+3)^{2}+4 & =3 \\\\ (x+3)^{2} & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(x+3)^{2}\\ge 0$ for all values of $x,$ there is no $x$ such that $(x+3)^{2}=-1.$ Therefore, circle has no $x$-intercepts, as shown below.\n\n![](images/cb/cb8a943a6f6cfd49e47c193f5379e06fd166cd17087c890233b42a7e1e7d2b89.png)" 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186092237 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396229 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186092351 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396216 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186092522 1068 #edb/ref 17592186089273 13194139533467 17592186045585 false] [17592186092522 1068 #edb/ref 17592186395991 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186092522 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186092522 1071 "Find all real eigenvalues of the matrix\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & 0 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$." 13194139533467 17592186045585 false] [17592186092522 1071 "Find all real eigenvalues of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3} & 0 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186092522 1074 "Let's start by writing the matrix $A-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3}-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 4 & 2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, let's find and solve the characteristic equation $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3}-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 4 & 2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (\\sqrt{3}-\\lambda )(2-\\lambda )-0\\cdot 4 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -\\sqrt{3})(\\lambda -2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda =\\sqrt{3}$ and $\\lambda =2.$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvalues of a triangular (or diagonal) matrix are the entries on the main diagonal." 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186096712 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396058 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186096714 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396065 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186096734 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396017 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186100919 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396031 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186105815 1038 #edb/ref 17592186395984 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186106041 1068 #edb/ref 17592186102102 13194139533467 17592186045585 false] [17592186106041 1068 #edb/ref 17592186396011 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186106041 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186106041 1071 "What linear transformation is represented by the following matrix\n$[\\begin{aligned}1 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]?$" 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186106041 1071 "What linear transformation is represented by the following matrix\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$?" 13194139533467 17592186045585 false] [17592186106041 1074 "We can find the images of the standard basis vectors $i$ and $j$ under the action of  $T,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(i) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=i \\\\ T(j) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=10i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix represents a *horizontal* shear with a shear factor of $10.$" 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186119777 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396044 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186120126 1068 #edb/ref 17592186116932 13194139533467 17592186045585 false] [17592186120126 1068 #edb/ref 17592186396038 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186120126 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186120126 1071 "How can we best describe the following matrix?\n\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}7 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533467 17592186045585 false] [17592186120126 1071 "How can we best describe the following matrix? \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186120126 1074 "Notice that $A$ is a matrix for which all entries above the diagonal are $0$'s:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, it is a lower triangular matrix.\n\nIt is not an upper triangular matrix or a diagonal matrix since there is a non-zero element below the diagonal: ${a}_{21}=3\\ne 0.$" 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186175954 1038 #edb/ref 17592186396051 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186395977 1068 #edb/ref 17592186395978 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186395977 1069 "q-10485" 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186395977 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186395977 1071 "Calculate the $x$-coordinates of the points where the hyperbola $\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}=1$ intersects the line $y=x.$" 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186395977 1072 #uuid "296c1691-3fdb-50aa-926f-7c3fb307cb51" 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186395977 1074 "To find the points where the hyperbola intersects the line $y=x,$ we first replace $y$ with $x$ in the equation of the hyperbola and solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{4{x}^{2}}{36}-\\frac{9{x}^{2}}{36} & =1 \\\\ -\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{36} & =1 \\\\ 5{x}^{2} & =-36\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe cannot take the square root of a negative number. Therefore, the equation has no real solutions. So, the hyperbola does not intersect the line $y=x.$" 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186395978 1075 #edb/ref 17592186395979 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186395978 1075 #edb/ref 17592186395980 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186395978 1075 #edb/ref 17592186395981 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186395978 1075 #edb/ref 17592186395982 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186395978 1075 #edb/ref 17592186395983 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186395978 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186395978 1077 "selection" 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186395978 1079 #edb/ref 17592186395981 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186395978 1080 #uuid "703485af-d39d-5b69-a26a-bac078d2ad7f" 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186395979 1081 "x=\\pm 2" 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186395979 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186395979 1084 #uuid "5420a5d8-e27c-54cd-8d4a-8807ed506a3f" 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186395980 1081 "x=\\pm 4" 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186395980 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186395980 1084 #uuid "b3896d35-3d39-5b8b-8500-59d135ad3570" 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186395981 1081 "There are no points of intersection" 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186395981 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186395981 1084 #uuid "6a6fdac5-53dc-5a87-9dff-5c99baea1ce1" 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186395982 1081 "x=\\pm 9" 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186395982 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186395982 1084 #uuid "54e5febe-e5c5-53fd-b8eb-4a5c7c0dad20" 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186395983 1081 "x=\\pm 3" 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186395983 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186395983 1084 #uuid "7aae46ab-d345-5f44-b157-efc8fa3bed1b" 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186395984 1068 #edb/ref 17592186395985 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186395984 1069 "q-10662" 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186395984 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186395984 1071 "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by \n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 5 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce $M$ to row echelon form, and then find the general solution of the linear system represented by the matrix." 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186395984 1072 #uuid "06de5100-449e-5af5-b65a-4e9e27bdf01a" 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186395984 1074 "Reducing $M$ to echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 5 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3 & -4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+y+z=2 \\\\ 0x+y-3z=1 \\\\ 0x+0y+0z=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince there are no such numbers $x,y,z$ that form a solution of the final equation $0x+0y+0z=-3,$ we can conclude that our system has no solution." 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186395985 1075 #edb/ref 17592186395986 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186395985 1075 #edb/ref 17592186395987 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186395985 1075 #edb/ref 17592186395988 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186395985 1075 #edb/ref 17592186395989 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186395985 1075 #edb/ref 17592186395990 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186395985 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186395985 1077 "selection" 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186395985 1079 #edb/ref 17592186395988 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186395985 1080 #uuid "2f5eaa46-bf95-53b6-ace1-3bd33b1c4991" 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186395986 1081 "[\\begin{aligned}2-y-z \\\\ 4-z \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" 13194