e] [17592186396236 1069 "q-9467" 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186396236 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186396236 1071 "Calculate the points where the ellipse $\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{9}=1$ intercepts the $y$-axis." 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186396236 1072 #uuid "abc24ff4-9a14-532c-930a-11e5cec7f6a6" 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186396236 1074 "To calculate the $y$-intercept, we need to substitute $x=0$ in the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $y.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{(x+2)}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{(y-3)}^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{(0+2)}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{(y-3)}^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{{(y-3)}^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ 12+{(y-3)}^{2} & =9 \\\\ {(y-3)}^{2} & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(y-3)^{2}\\ge 0$, there is no $y$ such that $(y-3)^{2}=-3.$ Therefore, the ellipse has no $y$-intercepts, meaning that it does not intercept the $y$-axis at any point." 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186396237 1075 #edb/ref 17592186396238 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186396237 1075 #edb/ref 17592186396239 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186396237 1075 #edb/ref 17592186396240 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186396237 1075 #edb/ref 17592186396241 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186396237 1075 #edb/ref 17592186396242 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186396237 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186396237 1077 "selection" 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186396237 1079 #edb/ref 17592186396240 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186396237 1080 #uuid "0e5117de-56c6-5e2d-bbc7-db8ca53a8c13" 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186396238 1081 "(0,1)" 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186396238 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533467 17592186045585 true] [17592186396238 1084 #uuid "dbace19d-5367-5c93-b9b0-ff6aeb65e1d